<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Sequential Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Why Autoregressive LLMs Are Slow</h2>
<p>Every mainstream LLM — GPT-4, Gemma, LLaMA — generates text the same way: one token at a time, left to right. To generate the word "Paris" in the sentence "The capital of France is Paris", the model runs a full forward pass over all prior tokens. Then it runs another full pass to generate "and". Then another for "the". Each new token requires its own GPU round-trip.</p>
<p>This creates a fundamental bottleneck: <strong>memory bandwidth, not compute</strong>. On each forward pass, the GPU must load the model weights (dozens of gigabytes for a large model) from HBM into compute units — but it only uses them to produce a handful of numbers (the logits for the next token). The GPU is <em>compute-starved</em>: its thousands of tensor cores sit idle while memory loads shuffle weights around. Peak GPU utilization for AR inference at small batch sizes is typically under 10%.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">ar-vs-diffusion-throughput</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click ▶ Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cv-ar" style="width:100%;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="ar-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="ar-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="canvas-label" id="ar-step-label">Step: 0 / 16</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The animation above shows the contrast. The <strong>autoregressive row</strong> lights up one token at a time, and the GPU utilization bar (bottom right) stays low — the chip is mostly waiting for memory. The <strong>diffusion row</strong> lights up all 16 tokens at once in iterative passes; the GPU bar maxes out. Both rows produce the same 16-token output, but diffusion takes 1–3 passes instead of 16 sequential ones.</p>

<p>DiffusionGemma is Google's attempt to break this bottleneck at scale. It's a 26B Mixture-of-Experts model (only 3.8B parameters active during inference) that generates text in 256-token parallel blocks using discrete diffusion — and achieves <strong>1,100+ tokens/sec</strong> on a single H100 at low batch sizes.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-diffusion" data-code="diffusion">
<div class="section-no">02 — Discrete Diffusion for Text</div>
<h2>Masking Forward, Denoising Back</h2>
<p>Diffusion models for images work by adding Gaussian noise to a pixel image, then learning to reverse that process. Text is different — tokens are discrete symbols, not continuous values. You can't add "a little noise" to the word "Paris". Instead, <strong>discrete diffusion</strong> uses <em>masking</em> as noise: the forward process randomly replaces tokens with a special <code>[MASK]</code> token, and the model learns to predict what the masked tokens should be.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Forward Process (masking)</div>
  <span class="math-var">q</span>(x<sub>t</sub> | x<sub>0</sub>) = mask each token independently with prob <span class="math-var">m</span>(t)
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">m</span>(t) increases from 0 → 1 as <span class="math-var">t</span>: 0 → T
  <span class="math-comment">  // t=0: no masking (original text); t=T: all tokens masked</span>
  <br><br>
  <div class="math-label">Reverse Process (denoising)</div>
  <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>θ</sub>(x<sub>0</sub> | x<sub>t</sub>) = predict original tokens from masked input
  <span class="math-comment">  // model sees context on both sides — bidirectional attention</span>
</div>

<p>The key difference from image diffusion: a text diffusion model sees the <em>entire partially-masked sequence at once</em> and uses <strong>bidirectional attention</strong> — it can look both left and right to predict masked tokens. An AR model only ever sees the tokens to its left. This bidirectionality is what makes tasks like code infilling, Sudoku, and constraint-heavy generation natural fits for diffusion.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">discrete-diffusion-denoising</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click ▶ Play — watch tokens emerge step by step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cv-denoise" style="width:100%;border-radius:6px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="dn-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="dn-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="canvas-label" id="dn-step-label">Step: 0 / 8</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Watch the sentence <em>"The capital of France is Paris and the Eiffel Tower stands at 330 metres"</em> emerge from a fully-masked canvas. High-confidence tokens (common words, proper nouns with unambiguous context) resolve first; uncertain positions keep iterating. By step 4–6 most tokens are settled; by step 8 the canvas is complete.</p>

<h3>Why 256 tokens at once?</h3>
<p>DiffusionGemma generates in <strong>256-token "canvases"</strong>. Each canvas runs its denoising loop independently, using bidirectional attention over the 256-token block. Once the canvas is complete, it's appended to the KV cache and the next canvas begins — a pattern called <strong>block-autoregressive generation</strong>. Within each canvas, all 256 tokens are generated in parallel; across canvases, generation is still sequential (each canvas depends on the previous). This is the key design choice: big enough blocks to amortize GPU utilization, small enough to maintain quality through bidirectional context.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — Canvas Architecture</div>
<h2>Encoder + Bidirectional Decoder</h2>
<p>DiffusionGemma is built on Gemma 4's 26B MoE backbone but uses an <strong>encoder-decoder design</strong> — a significant departure from standard decoder-only LLMs. The encoder prefills the prompt using standard causal attention, building a KV cache. The decoder then operates on each 256-token canvas using <em>bidirectional</em> (non-causal) attention.</p>

