<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-eb" data-code="eb">
<div class="section-no">04 — Entropy-Bounded Denoising</div>
<h2>Tokens Lock In by Confidence</h2>
<p>The denoising sampler is the heart of DiffusionGemma. At each step, the decoder predicts a full distribution over the vocabulary for every masked canvas position. The question is: which tokens should be <em>committed</em> (locked) and which should be <em>re-masked</em> for the next iteration?</p>

<p>DiffusionGemma uses <strong>Entropy-Bounded (EB) denoising</strong>: at each step, only tokens whose predicted distribution has sufficiently low entropy (high confidence) are locked in. Specifically, the sampler selects the lowest-entropy positions where the mutual information bound remains below an entropy threshold of 0.1. All other tokens are fully re-masked — not partially noised, but completely reset to [MASK].</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">EB Denoising Decision Rule</div>
  lock token <span class="math-var">i</span> if: <span class="math-var">H</span>(p<sub>i</sub>) &lt; ε <span class="math-comment">  // entropy below bound ε = 0.1</span>
  <br>
  re-mask token <span class="math-var">i</span> if: <span class="math-var">H</span>(p<sub>i</sub>) ≥ ε <span class="math-comment">  // uncertain → try again</span>
  <br><br>
  temperature: linear decay <span class="math-var">τ</span>: 0.8 → 0.4 over denoising steps
  <br>
  early stop if: avg_entropy &lt; 0.005 <strong>and</strong> top-1 tokens stable for 2 steps
</div>

<p>This creates a natural "easy tokens first" curriculum: common words in unambiguous positions (articles, punctuation, proper nouns with clear context) lock in immediately; structurally constrained tokens (the middle of a long word, a number that must satisfy a constraint) may take many steps. Simpler tasks like structured code generation can complete in far fewer than 48 steps, giving DiffusionGemma <em>dynamic throughput</em> — easy requests are proportionally faster.</p>

