<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §06 -->
<section id="s-usecases" data-code="usecases">
<div class="section-no">06 — When Diffusion Wins</div>
<h2>Right Tool for the Right Task</h2>
<p>DiffusionGemma isn't a universal replacement for autoregressive models — it's a tool with a specific performance profile. Understanding when to reach for it requires thinking about both the <em>task structure</em> and the <em>deployment context</em>.</p>

<h3>Task structure</h3>
<p>Diffusion's bidirectionality gives it an inherent advantage on tasks where the answer constraints are distributed across the output — not just left-to-right. A few key examples:</p>

<div class="use-matrix">
  <div class="use-card diff-wins">
    <h4>✓ Diffusion wins</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Code infilling (fill in the middle given before + after)</li>
      <li>Constraint-satisfaction (Sudoku, crosswords, structured generation)</li>
      <li>In-line editing (modify text while preserving surrounding context)</li>
      <li>Amino acid sequence design (mutual constraints throughout)</li>
      <li>Short-form generation under real-time latency budgets</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="use-card ar-wins">
    <h4>⚠ Autoregressive wins</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Long chain-of-thought reasoning (each step depends on previous)</li>
      <li>High-accuracy math / coding competitions (AIME gap is large)</li>
      <li>Cloud API serving at high throughput (large batch sizes)</li>
      <li>Vision-heavy tasks (vision benchmarks show a significant gap)</li>
      <li>Applications where output quality is non-negotiable</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Deployment context</h3>
<p>The 4× speedup is hardware-specific. DiffusionGemma moves the bottleneck from <em>memory bandwidth</em> to <em>compute</em>. On architectures with unified memory (Apple Silicon M-series, where memory bandwidth and compute are more balanced), the advantage shrinks. On discrete GPUs with high compute-to-bandwidth ratios (H100, RTX 5090), the advantage is strongest.</p>

<p>The model fits within 18 GB VRAM when quantized — accessible on a consumer RTX 4090 or 5090 — and the 3.8B active parameter footprint means each forward pass is relatively cheap despite the large total parameter count.</p>

<h3>Thinking mode</h3>
<p>DiffusionGemma supports a <code>&lt;|think|&gt;</code> prefix for extended reasoning. When enabled, the model emits a <code>&lt;|channel&gt;thought</code> block before the final answer — structurally similar to chain-of-thought prompting. Interestingly, this thinking happens within the same canvas-diffusion framework, so even the reasoning tokens are generated in parallel blocks rather than left-to-right. Whether this gives a systematic advantage over AR thinking is an open question.</p>

<h3>Apache 2.0 and what it signals</h3>
<p>Google released DiffusionGemma under Apache 2.0 — the same permissive license as Gemma 4. This is a clear signal: diffusion-based LLMs are ready for production experimentation, and Google wants to accelerate the ecosystem's exploration of this space. The explicit "experimental" framing and the honest benchmarking (DiffusionGemma is behind on most standard metrics) suggest this is the beginning of a research direction rather than a production flagship. Future versions, trained with more diffusion-specific data and larger canvas sizes, may close the quality gap while keeping the speed advantage.</p>
</section>

